-- ---------- Paper ----------
VALUES ('21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 'ccs-practice-2', 'CCS Practice Paper 2', 'ccs', ARRAY['ICD-10-CM', 'ICD-10-PCS', 'Outpatient Coding', 'ED Coding', 'PSI/HAC', 'RAC Audits', 'Provider Queries', 'HIPAA', 'EHR Interoperability']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 2, 240);

-- ── Section: Domain 1 — Coding Knowledge and Skills (40 questions) — Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank. ──

VALUES ('3f5992c7-c724-4108-b707-e7e312f99178', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 1, 'A patient presents to the Emergency Department with chest pain; workup rules out MI, and the final ED diagnosis is ''chest pain, etiology undetermined.'' Per outpatient/ED coding guidelines, the appropriate first-listed diagnosis is:');
VALUES ('3f5992c7-c724-4108-b707-e7e312f99178', '700c3ba9-8948-4ad0-bdc7-139f01463e1a', 'Outpatient/ED coding never applies the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis rule — when a suspected condition is ruled out and no more definitive diagnosis is reached, the documented signs/symptoms are coded, not a presumed or ruled-out condition.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('cb1131c4-1775-4319-adcd-cd03f8dbbf31', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 2, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''cutting into a body part, without taking out any of the body part, to release it from an abnormal physical constraint''?');
VALUES ('cb1131c4-1775-4319-adcd-cd03f8dbbf31', '16494dc2-7d47-499d-858f-72bc8c73245b', 'Release frees a body part from an abnormal physical constraint (e.g., adhesion, scar tissue) by cutting/separating, without taking out any tissue — distinguishing it from Division, which is used to separate/transect a body part when no other root operation applies (and always without a device left behind).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5fda6183-9124-47ed-b395-9a26ac72a4bf', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 3, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''cutting into or through a body part, without draining fluids/gases, in order to separate/transect it,'' used only when no other root operation applies (e.g., a spinal cordotomy)?');
VALUES ('5fda6183-9124-47ed-b395-9a26ac72a4bf', '05f0142e-698b-4aeb-a262-d81bfb8810d2', 'Division is a residual/''not elsewhere classified'' root operation for cutting/separating body part tissue with no device left behind and no other specific root operation being a better fit.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('27f8bdff-55de-415d-b15b-f74e8e8f09a0', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 4, 'A patient undergoes an outpatient screening colonoscopy that identifies and removes a polyp via snare technique during the same encounter. Coding guidance for outpatient screening procedures that become therapeutic generally directs:');
VALUES ('27f8bdff-55de-415d-b15b-f74e8e8f09a0', '59446b88-f4f4-45c4-a318-2f61ecd63892', 'When a screening procedure becomes diagnostic/therapeutic in the same encounter, coding should reflect both what was intended (screening) and what was actually found/done (the polyp and its removal) — dropping either the screening context or the actual finding is a common but avoidable outpatient coding gap.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('dec71dd4-fb94-48b5-bbbf-4c7611bed795', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 5, 'Per UHDDS ''other diagnoses'' (secondary diagnosis) reporting for INPATIENT stays, which of these would most clearly meet the definition of a reportable additional diagnosis?');
VALUES ('dec71dd4-fb94-48b5-bbbf-4c7611bed795', '13ff274c-1ffa-49b1-a1c4-0724727824d1', 'A new condition actively diagnosed and treated during the stay clearly meets UHDDS''s clinical-evaluation/therapeutic-treatment criteria for a reportable additional diagnosis — passing historical mentions, family history, and allergy-list entries alone do not.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('e2aaa6a3-03e9-4641-a709-cf5bd0fa6b27', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 6, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation involves ''taking out solid matter, fluids, or gases from a body part by use of force''?');
VALUES ('e2aaa6a3-03e9-4641-a709-cf5bd0fa6b27', '011a18da-e82f-4664-8f4e-b1987de320ed', 'Extirpation takes out solid matter (e.g., a thrombus, calculus, or foreign body) that has no natural or created channel to the outside of the body — distinguishing it from Extraction (pulling/stripping a body part itself) and Drainage (taking out fluids/gases).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5e237299-89c4-4ff6-82c2-a70434be820c', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 7, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''taking or letting out fluids and/or gases from a body part''?');
VALUES ('5e237299-89c4-4ff6-82c2-a70434be820c', '2dd30a42-8e2b-401c-819f-78552ead2c48', 'Drainage removes fluids/gases (e.g., paracentesis, chest tube placement) — the qualifier ''diagnostic'' distinguishes a purely diagnostic drainage procedure (e.g., diagnostic thoracentesis) from a therapeutic one.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('abec1d9a-48bb-4dd1-af05-d52658ed4145', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 8, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation is defined as ''breaking solid matter in a body part into pieces,'' where the resulting pieces are not taken out?');
VALUES ('abec1d9a-48bb-4dd1-af05-d52658ed4145', 'e590212f-2a4b-41f5-8730-9b500afe1eb2', 'Fragmentation breaks solid matter (e.g., a kidney stone via lithotripsy) into pieces that are then eliminated naturally or by other means, without the pieces being physically taken out during the fragmentation procedure itself.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b749a449-667b-4524-93e7-db379788d5b7', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 9, 'A physician documents ''urosepsis'' as the sole diagnosis for an inpatient stay. Per coding guidance on this term, the coder should generally:');
VALUES ('b749a449-667b-4524-93e7-db379788d5b7', '0ac86178-1a4f-445f-9737-c15f46b8d9bb', '''Urosepsis'' is a classic example of a nonspecific term coding guidance flags as needing physician clarification — it is used inconsistently across clinicians to mean different things (UTI alone, or true sepsis due to UTI), so query rather than assumption is the compliant path.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b9e9b540-be2f-41df-9405-a306a0de83db', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 10, 'In the outpatient hospital setting, when a patient is seen for a scheduled procedure that is later canceled after the patient arrives but before the procedure begins, coding guidance generally directs:');
VALUES ('b9e9b540-be2f-41df-9405-a306a0de83db', 'c7e40a62-9cf8-4e0b-8073-8e58952b57c4', 'Coding always reflects what actually happened during the encounter — a canceled procedure should never be coded as performed; the reason for the visit and the fact of the cancellation (via appropriate coding conventions) are what get reported.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('fe58af0e-0ee8-44e5-86dc-5e730b2f9e03', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 11, 'A patient''s health record documents ''acute blood loss anemia'' following a surgical procedure with significant intraoperative blood loss, explicitly linked by the surgeon''s postoperative note. This would most appropriately be coded as:');
VALUES ('fe58af0e-0ee8-44e5-86dc-5e730b2f9e03', '9108af1d-d45e-4e87-9582-ae32d5203938', 'Explicitly-documented, clinically significant acute blood loss anemia — especially with a provider-established link to the procedure — is coded per the postoperative-complication documentation rule discussed earlier: it requires the provider''s own established relationship, which this scenario provides.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('85ced809-3b92-4c8f-94a0-877e40924266', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 12, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation describes ''exchanging all or a portion of a body part for a similar, natural or synthetic body part that physically takes the place of that body part,'' using a qualifier to indicate an autograft/allograft/syngeneic/zooplastic source or synthetic substitute?');
VALUES ('85ced809-3b92-4c8f-94a0-877e40924266', '18ae78e5-4bf7-41e2-973e-5da2cf9d92f9', 'Transplantation is specifically for putting in a living body part taken from another individual or animal to physically take the place of/function for a similar body part (e.g., kidney or liver transplant) — a related but distinct concept from Replacement, which more broadly includes synthetic/nonliving substitutes as well.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('57fc09b3-4ade-4f22-bfb0-182281fbf0ca', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 13, 'A coder is assigning ICD-10-PCS codes for a multi-vessel coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) using both a saphenous vein graft and a left internal mammary artery (LIMA) graft. Which coding principle is most relevant here?');
VALUES ('57fc09b3-4ade-4f22-bfb0-182281fbf0ca', 'b97c71f8-f5f3-453c-8d57-80b8889dc51b', 'ICD-10-PCS bypass procedures use the qualifier character to specify the type of conduit/graft material, and coding multiple distinct graft types/target vessels appropriately may require more than one code — a detail that trips up coders unfamiliar with the qualifier''s role in bypass coding specifically.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('e3e2b10c-c315-4870-850c-8569d2cf9699', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 14, 'A patient is admitted with a stroke and, during the stay, develops aspiration pneumonia clearly linked by physician documentation to dysphagia caused by the stroke. Sequencing-wise:');
VALUES ('e3e2b10c-c315-4870-850c-8569d2cf9699', '814bf050-717a-457f-80da-21cd493db15f', 'The stroke remains the reason the admission was occasioned; the aspiration pneumonia — a condition arising during the stay — is an additional diagnosis, appropriately linked per documentation, not an automatic override of the principal diagnosis determination.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('0f1a7c14-ebb5-4432-9865-daae7937cd14', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 15, 'The ''seventh character'' extension requirement in ICD-10-CM applies to which of the following code categories, among others?');
VALUES ('0f1a7c14-ebb5-4432-9865-daae7937cd14', '7d2fa6f5-5d7c-4d08-b4b4-98a1240d2d26', 'Seventh-character extensions are required only for specific code categories/chapters that define them (injury/poisoning and certain external causes, obstetrics, certain fracture/musculoskeletal codes, etc.) — not universally across all of ICD-10-CM.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a98ad227-4e63-454d-b9aa-de912a2c6a5b', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 16, 'When a 7th-character-requiring code does not have enough characters to accommodate the extension in its correct position, coding convention requires:');
VALUES ('a98ad227-4e63-454d-b9aa-de912a2c6a5b', 'ff103c1b-3f5f-402f-ad33-ff0c67407afc', 'ICD-10-CM''s placeholder ''X'' convention exists precisely for this — filling empty character positions so the required 7th-character extension lands correctly, a frequently tested mechanical coding-convention detail.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7531bb6c-8961-4ac3-92ab-cf66fdb893bd', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 17, 'A patient has a diagnosis documented only as ''morbid obesity'' with a specific BMI value also charted by a clinician (e.g., a dietitian) elsewhere in the record. Per coding guidelines, BMI codes:');
VALUES ('7531bb6c-8961-4ac3-92ab-cf66fdb893bd', '8877f678-006b-453a-b6ad-3ef0e36f6b5a', 'Official Guidelines carve out a specific exception allowing BMI, certain depth-of-non-pressure-ulcer/pressure-ulcer-stage documentation, and a few similar data points to be taken from non-physician clinician documentation, as long as the underlying diagnosis itself is provider-documented — a nuance that distinguishes it from the general documentation-source rule.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7905071c-31ef-481d-8de6-5d807f3418bd', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 18, 'Which of the following would be the most appropriate use of an ICD-10-CM ''unspecified'' code?');
VALUES ('7905071c-31ef-481d-8de6-5d807f3418bd', 'b0048c9d-19d4-47d8-a980-57f6df0b670d', 'Unspecified codes exist for genuine situations where the record doesn''t support greater specificity and that specificity isn''t reasonably obtainable — they are not meant to be a default convenience when specific information is actually available or query-able.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('9f6ff26e-84af-4a35-a3fa-274c75710bcc', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 19, 'A patient undergoes an ICD-10-PCS-coded procedure described as ''putting in nonliving biological or synthetic material that reinforces or augments the function of a portion of a body part.'' Which root operation is this?');
VALUES ('9f6ff26e-84af-4a35-a3fa-274c75710bcc', '36bab092-1cf8-4d96-971f-40eff884b190', 'Supplement reinforces/augments a body part''s own function using nonliving biological (e.g., cadaver tissue) or synthetic material without physically taking the place of the body part — the classic example is hernia repair with mesh.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('5f851de2-a339-468b-8333-8dbabd1724ed', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 20, 'A hernia repair using surgical mesh is generally coded using which ICD-10-PCS root operation, reflecting the mesh''s function of reinforcing the repaired tissue?');
VALUES ('5f851de2-a339-468b-8333-8dbabd1724ed', '5be8a2dd-7968-4bb6-b3b3-f4e552831ad3', 'Mesh used to reinforce a hernia repair is coded as Supplement, since the mesh augments/reinforces the existing body part''s structure/function rather than replacing it entirely or simply being inserted as a monitoring/functional device (Insertion) or being a not-elsewhere-classified restoration technique (Repair).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('0867cfa3-25bd-492a-8099-386be602561b', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 21, 'A patient is coded with acute respiratory failure as principal diagnosis following documentation review confirming it was the condition chiefly responsible for the admission, with pneumonia as a contributing secondary diagnosis. Per coding guidelines for respiratory failure sequencing when it is due to another condition also present on admission:');
VALUES ('0867cfa3-25bd-492a-8099-386be602561b', '92c1f486-31a7-4f52-8b27-31cef29018be', 'There is specific guideline language addressing acute respiratory failure sequencing: it CAN be principal diagnosis when supported by the UHDDS definition, even with a comorbid condition like pneumonia also present — a commonly tested nuance since many assume respiratory failure is ''always secondary.''', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('46ed7483-4457-4b8d-91a4-608492c96160', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 22, 'A patient''s documentation states ''sepsis, resolved'' as a historical note during a subsequent, unrelated admission with no active infection this time. This history should be coded, if at all, using:');
VALUES ('46ed7483-4457-4b8d-91a4-608492c96160', 'ec0b763f-8963-4b53-83a9-f34cf508755f', 'A resolved condition affecting current care/risk assessment is appropriately captured with a personal history Z code, not an active-disease code — conflating ''history of'' with ''currently has'' is a common but avoidable sequencing/code-selection error.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b202c230-6a20-42fc-8d50-060e2f731750', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 23, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation describes ''taking out or off a device from a body part''?');
VALUES ('b202c230-6a20-42fc-8d50-060e2f731750', '7f5b2041-bddd-4abe-925e-4ede823b7bfd', 'Removal takes a device out of/off a body part, with no replacement device put in during the same procedure (if a replacement device is put in, that portion may instead be coded as Change, Insertion, or another appropriate root operation depending on technique).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('11de4ddd-56eb-4252-b93a-24e709a61b10', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 24, 'Which ICD-10-PCS root operation describes ''taking out a device and putting in an identical or similar device without cutting or puncturing the skin or a mucous membrane'' (i.e., via an existing orifice or previously created opening)?');
VALUES ('11de4ddd-56eb-4252-b93a-24e709a61b10', '1edab567-e8ae-4e79-b981-971aad13ce34', 'Change is specifically for external, non-invasive device exchanges through an existing opening (e.g., a routine urinary catheter change or wound vac dressing change), distinct from Revision (correcting a malfunctioning device) or Removal (taking a device out with no replacement).', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('1915faaa-0097-4ac8-bfd7-13cfbddff432', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 25, 'In outpatient coding, when multiple coexisting chronic conditions are documented but not specifically addressed or evaluated during the visit (e.g., stable, well-controlled hypertension noted on a problem list during a visit for an unrelated acute ankle sprain), general coding practice is:');
VALUES ('1915faaa-0097-4ac8-bfd7-13cfbddff432', '8a19be43-df24-4fb1-8d8a-b9d5bce3ae4f', 'This is a genuinely nuanced, guideline-and-policy-dependent area (distinguishing ''affects care'' from ''unaddressed history'') — the key test is clinical relevance/impact on the encounter''s management, not blanket inclusion or blanket exclusion of every chronic condition on a problem list.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('a76d68b6-7b2c-45f0-8d53-d3f39c0e236c', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 26, 'A patient with type 2 diabetes is admitted for treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). Which coding structure applies?');
VALUES ('a76d68b6-7b2c-45f0-8d53-d3f39c0e236c', '2c0070f6-cb80-4f60-839e-dd17029d4c9e', 'ICD-10-CM''s diabetes mellitus codes include specific combination codes for DKA (with and without coma) under both type 1 and type 2 diabetes — DKA is a recognized acute complication classified directly within the diabetes code structure.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('616bac63-0037-465f-9ff7-b2e4e2fb279a', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 27, 'Which of the following best reflects correct use of the ''code first'' instructional note?');
VALUES ('616bac63-0037-465f-9ff7-b2e4e2fb279a', '2a93ef6b-9d88-4df9-8f1f-3626fcb0597c', '''Code first'' notes enforce mandatory sequencing — typically appearing on manifestation codes that require an underlying-etiology code to be sequenced first, since the manifestation code alone doesn''t fully represent the condition without its cause.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('937ebdee-443c-439e-9780-2c3e5aa481b9', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 28, 'The ''use additional code'' instructional note, as opposed to ''code first,'' indicates:');
VALUES ('937ebdee-443c-439e-9780-2c3e5aa481b9', 'de8d0f8b-7327-4ded-a825-326f58c28f08', '''Use additional code'' typically appears on the underlying/etiology code, directing the coder to add a further code (e.g., a manifestation) after it — the mirror-image instructional pairing to ''code first,'' which appears on the code that must be sequenced second.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4b606836-1685-4539-b0cb-e376859c9564', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 29, 'A patient with chronic systolic heart failure is admitted for an acute exacerbation of that same heart failure. Correct ICD-10-CM coding requires:');
VALUES ('4b606836-1685-4539-b0cb-e376859c9564', '86a76c88-b70f-4b3a-b854-6e745481c780', 'ICD-10-CM''s heart failure codes are structured to capture acuity (acute, chronic, acute on chronic) together with type (systolic, diastolic, combined) when documented — using only a generic or single-acuity code when more specific documentation exists under-codes the encounter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('826e846a-9bd8-47c2-a704-4cc78dbf5a92', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 30, 'Which of the following is the most accurate statement about coding ''personal history of'' versus ''family history of'' Z codes?');
VALUES ('826e846a-9bd8-47c2-a704-4cc78dbf5a92', '5e6f3493-1ae3-42da-bba5-aaacfd04fcbc', 'These two Z code categories are not interchangeable — mixing up ''personal'' (the patient''s own past condition) and ''family'' (a relative''s condition, relevant to the patient''s risk) misrepresents a clinically meaningful distinction.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('705d5613-ee0b-4a5f-8833-b54fc685719b', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 31, 'A patient is admitted with a fracture and the documentation specifies it is a ''pathological fracture due to osteoporosis,'' sustained with minimal trauma. Coding guidance for pathological fractures directs:');
VALUES ('705d5613-ee0b-4a5f-8833-b54fc685719b', '24b0db15-a82f-455a-93f5-72400e5eea86', 'Pathological fractures (from an underlying disease process like osteoporosis, with minimal or no trauma) are classified in ICD-10-CM using a distinct code category with its own conventions — a different classification structure than traumatic fracture codes, a detail commonly missed by coders defaulting to traumatic fracture logic.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('7aa7da7f-dc4f-4417-a12e-451dc1a3dbf7', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 32, 'Which best describes correct handling of a ''late effect''/sequela diagnosis, such as ''dysphagia due to old cerebrovascular accident,'' in ICD-10-CM?');
VALUES ('7aa7da7f-dc4f-4417-a12e-451dc1a3dbf7', 'cce290a7-362e-4820-b4b6-4d710b62cd89', 'Sequela (late effect) coding generally sequences the residual condition first, with the cause-of-the-sequela code following — the exact reverse of how the original acute event and its immediate complications would have been sequenced during the acute encounter.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('ef334e70-834b-4185-bf73-a4d6795d65bb', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 33, 'A malignant neoplasm is documented as having ''metastasized to bone and liver'' with the primary site ''unknown/unable to be determined'' after diagnostic workup. Coding guidance for a primary malignancy of unknown site directs:');
VALUES ('ef334e70-834b-4185-bf73-a4d6795d65bb', 'db56c5ce-2f47-4cc5-ad20-df563ee59fb9', 'When a primary malignancy''s site genuinely cannot be determined despite workup, ICD-10-CM provides a code for malignant neoplasm of unspecified site to be used alongside the specific metastatic site codes — an appropriate use of an ''unspecified'' code when specificity is genuinely not obtainable, not a documentation failure requiring an endless query loop.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('57b4aa52-54b9-43be-9e65-70715456e3d0', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 34, 'Which of the following ICD-10-CM guideline concepts governs how ''impending'' or ''threatened'' conditions (e.g., ''impending MI'') should be coded?');
VALUES ('57b4aa52-54b9-43be-9e65-70715456e3d0', '8bdab718-9eab-4f82-9a6a-167b23500ded', '''Impending'' and ''threatened'' conditions are addressed by their own specific Alphabetic Index entries and generally follow logic similar to the inpatient uncertain-diagnosis guideline in the appropriate setting — this is a nuanced area coders should recognize has explicit Index guidance rather than assuming a blanket rule.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('b30b2fb0-3683-4639-922a-64dd41adfc1c', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 35, 'A patient''s discharge diagnosis list includes ''anemia,'' with no further specification anywhere in the record despite a full workup being documented (labs, iron studies, etc., all reviewed by the physician) that would support a more specific type. In this scenario, the correct approach is most likely:');
VALUES ('b30b2fb0-3683-4639-922a-64dd41adfc1c', '6ed1626c-af63-49c8-83f6-8208450d7a5b', 'Before defaulting to unspecified or firing off a query, a careful coder reviews all available clinical documentation (including workup/labs already interpreted by the physician) — genuine specificity may already be supportable from the full record even without a single explicit summary term, and only a real remaining gap warrants a query.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('4e34dee5-b78d-45b0-88c1-7d4e0e8ea75d', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 36, 'Which of the following best describes the correct use of ICD-10-CM external cause codes (V, W, X, Y codes)?');
VALUES ('4e34dee5-b78d-45b0-88c1-7d4e0e8ea75d', '7aa6101a-c0ff-4ab9-bfee-ec80eae469a1', 'External cause codes are supplemental — they describe the circumstances of an injury/condition for data-collection purposes but are never sequenced as the principal/first-listed diagnosis; the actual injury/condition code fills that role.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('3f527a0b-5dec-44d8-93da-9a6f723210b1', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 37, 'A hospital record for a patient admitted with acute pancreatitis documents no clear etiology despite typical workup (no gallstones on imaging, no reported alcohol use, normal lipids). Per coding guidance for cases where etiology genuinely cannot be established:');
VALUES ('3f527a0b-5dec-44d8-93da-9a6f723210b1', '8356eeb7-c5e5-421b-9384-4980afb31d90', 'When no specific etiology is clinically established despite reasonable workup, coding to the level of specificity the documentation actually supports (which may be an unspecified or ''without mention of'' code, or a specific ''idiopathic'' code if that term is actually used) is correct and expected — not a coding failure.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('d4a0bfb2-fb13-405f-99f2-db130b8d5eed', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 38, 'Which of the following best describes how ICD-10-CM classifies ''stage'' for chronic kidney disease (CKD)?');
VALUES ('d4a0bfb2-fb13-405f-99f2-db130b8d5eed', '44c7bbb3-749e-4bcc-a668-5c1c36c1570b', 'ICD-10-CM''s CKD codes include distinct stage-specific options (including ESRD) — using an unspecified CKD code when the record documents a specific stage under-codes the severity that''s actually supported by documentation.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('32199abc-ab3a-4ca4-9842-51409da1c561', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 39, 'A patient''s admission is for ''observation status'' that is later converted to inpatient status during the same hospital stay. From a coding/UHDDS standpoint, the encounter is generally treated as:');
VALUES ('32199abc-ab3a-4ca4-9842-51409da1c561', '99c5b19e-9717-4f39-9ec5-84ce4e7dbe74', 'Once observation status converts to a formal inpatient admission, UHDDS/inpatient coding principles apply to the stay as a single inpatient encounter, with principal diagnosis reflecting the whole course of care leading to the admission — not artificially fragmented at the status-change point.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

VALUES ('898d2235-1484-4b49-9bf9-a58fbd330dd9', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 40, 'A patient is admitted for treatment of dehydration and, during the workup, is found to have an underlying malignancy that will require ongoing treatment after this stay, but is not itself treated during this admission. Per UHDDS principal diagnosis selection, which is most likely correct?');
VALUES ('898d2235-1484-4b49-9bf9-a58fbd330dd9', 'eda9aa78-350c-41ff-a1e0-713888e02857', 'UHDDS principal diagnosis selection is anchored to what occasioned THIS specific admission after study — a more severe or chronic condition discovered incidentally doesn''t automatically displace the actual reason for the admission as principal diagnosis.', 'Domain 1 (Coding Knowledge & Skills) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and the ICD-10-CM/PCS Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting; not sourced from an official AHIMA exam question bank.');

-- ── Section: Domain 2 — Coding Documentation (20 questions) — Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance. ──

VALUES ('0a60b89e-f9b2-492f-9946-9d54a2b97686', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 41, 'A same-day surgery record documents a preoperative diagnosis of ''possible appendicitis'' and a postoperative diagnosis of ''acute appendicitis, confirmed on pathology.'' For outpatient coding of the surgical encounter, which diagnosis governs?');
VALUES ('0a60b89e-f9b2-492f-9946-9d54a2b97686', 'bea6c880-d989-409a-b13d-6042fc13e6e7', 'Outpatient surgical coding uses the most definitive diagnosis known at the time of coding — a confirmed postoperative/pathology diagnosis supersedes an uncertain preoperative impression, since outpatient guidelines direct coding to the highest degree of certainty documented.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('d80bd6fe-3a64-474d-b571-5bb84bf47802', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 42, 'A record contains a typed history and physical stating ''no known drug allergies'' alongside a nursing allergy band and MAR both documenting a penicillin allergy. This conflicting documentation is most relevant to which task?');
VALUES ('d80bd6fe-3a64-474d-b571-5bb84bf47802', '08e6f694-6b5c-40d2-ad6f-c722d67ff1ad', 'A clinically significant conflict like this (patient safety implications for allergy status) is squarely a documentation-integrity issue requiring resolution — though it isn''t primarily a coding issue in itself, HIM/coding staff encountering such conflicts as part of chart review should recognize and appropriately escalate them.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('3a83c977-7875-4304-9417-caf493a91da7', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 43, 'Which best reflects appropriate handling when a coder finds that required procedure documentation exists, but is scattered across multiple disjointed notes (a brief op note stub, a separate detailed dictation completed days later, and an addendum) rather than one consolidated report?');
VALUES ('3a83c977-7875-4304-9417-caf493a91da7', 'cd9af9f0-4893-49e4-a893-9ed806b14327', 'Coding is based on the complete, legitimately authenticated record for the episode — a coder should review all relevant documentation (main note, later dictation, addenda) rather than requiring one single document or ignoring later, more detailed documentation.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('99209422-ef8f-40b5-abd0-6501025dad4d', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 44, 'A CDI/coding-documentation review identifies a physician consistently using the abbreviation ''CP'' in notes without clarifying whether it means ''chest pain'' or ''cerebral palsy'' for a given patient. This ambiguity is best addressed at the documentation-integrity level by:');
VALUES ('99209422-ef8f-40b5-abd0-6501025dad4d', 'e8b1e2d7-357c-43f1-b746-007822f42883', 'Ambiguous abbreviations should be resolved using surrounding clinical context first, with a compliant query for a genuinely unclear individual case — and a recurring pattern like this is also a systemic documentation-quality issue worth flagging beyond the single encounter.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('6547bbeb-fb52-4a20-8059-cbb490cb127a', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 45, 'Which of the following best supports validating that a coded present-on-admission indicator of ''N'' (not present on admission) is accurate?');
VALUES ('6547bbeb-fb52-4a20-8059-cbb490cb127a', 'e5180d29-c5f1-4c6d-bc2e-dcdfb256991c', 'Accurate POA validation requires reviewing actual admission-time clinical documentation for evidence of the condition''s presence or absence — not defaulting based on absence from a single note or the mere order in which a problem list was updated, both of which can be misleading proxies for true clinical onset.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('31e0d10d-0e0d-4150-b981-894f8b3b7bb9', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 46, 'A discharge summary is dictated and electronically signed by a resident physician, with no co-signature from the supervising attending anywhere in the chart, at a facility whose medical staff bylaws require attending co-signature for discharge summaries. From a documentation-completeness standpoint, this is:');
VALUES ('31e0d10d-0e0d-4150-b981-894f8b3b7bb9', '920119d6-4be9-4346-a02f-0ada26eafb0b', 'Authentication requirements set by facility bylaws/accreditation standards are a real documentation-completeness dimension — a missing required co-signature is a gap to be identified and remediated, not something to either ignore or treat as automatically invalidating the clinical content.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('cc4a6ecf-0860-4279-ba86-de30880fbcd8', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 47, 'A patient''s vital signs flowsheet (nursing-entered, structured EHR data) shows a sustained fever and tachycardia over 12 hours, but no physician note anywhere in the chart during that window addresses or interprets these findings. This represents:');
VALUES ('cc4a6ecf-0860-4279-ba86-de30880fbcd8', 'd9792e32-93e4-45f7-ae10-585079df4df6', 'Objective structured data (vitals) suggesting a clinically significant, undocumented event is a recognized query-opportunity/documentation-gap pattern — it doesn''t automatically mean a condition exists, but it''s a legitimate signal worth reviewing rather than ignoring.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('81389433-5120-4a16-bcbc-8c86a85e3c29', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 48, 'Which best illustrates ''ensuring documentation supports the specificity of an assigned ICD-10-PCS device value'' (e.g., drug-eluting vs. non-drug-eluting stent)?');
VALUES ('81389433-5120-4a16-bcbc-8c86a85e3c29', 'd99b4775-4358-4acb-82e3-65d0c89eb685', 'Device-specific ICD-10-PCS values (e.g., drug-eluting intraluminal device) require actual clinical/operative documentation support — assuming a common device type without confirming the actual documented device risks an inaccurate code, and a charge-master entry alone isn''t equivalent to clinical documentation validation.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('5e4cd141-a975-48b5-8442-3125869eed82', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 49, 'A physician''s progress note documents ''patient doing well, will discuss further at follow-up'' regarding a previously flagged abnormal finding, without ever actually stating whether the finding was clinically significant, resolved, or still under evaluation. This ambiguous closure is best handled by:');
VALUES ('5e4cd141-a975-48b5-8442-3125869eed82', '8b12830e-a485-435f-8f65-73dc83c8a366', 'Vague, non-committal documentation language doesn''t equate to an actual clinical determination — a coder shouldn''t infer resolution or insignificance from tone alone when code assignment genuinely depends on knowing the actual clinical status.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('00b35e7c-84a3-4e58-8c74-e2ef05cab101', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 50, 'Which of the following best reflects a compliant approach to ''cloned'' or templated EHR documentation review for coding purposes?');
VALUES ('00b35e7c-84a3-4e58-8c74-e2ef05cab101', 'cbc16348-42f9-4cde-9cd2-b710aef26b00', 'Cloned/copy-forward documentation is a recognized EHR-era documentation-integrity risk — the concern isn''t templates themselves, but verifying that carried-forward content genuinely reflects the current encounter rather than stale, outdated information.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('b03790af-bb8c-44b4-9d21-83586f2cd87e', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 51, 'A record shows a physician order for ''insulin sliding scale'' and nursing documentation of insulin administration, but no physician note anywhere documents a diagnosis of diabetes or hyperglycemia. This scenario illustrates:');
VALUES ('b03790af-bb8c-44b4-9d21-83586f2cd87e', '5403d655-a14d-430e-a6ee-9c26d410972b', 'Treatment being given (even clearly, like an insulin order) does not by itself establish a codeable diagnosis without physician documentation of the underlying condition — this is a classic ''treatment without a stated diagnosis'' documentation gap requiring further review or query, not an assumption of a specific diabetes type.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('18575f6c-16ea-4b53-a446-2e7b59fbf29c', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 52, 'Which best describes an appropriate documentation-integrity check specifically for procedures involving implantable devices subject to recall or safety monitoring?');
VALUES ('18575f6c-16ea-4b53-a446-2e7b59fbf29c', '883e33d2-9e51-46b5-a919-2f839d604e2d', 'Implant documentation completeness serves both accurate device-value coding and broader patient-safety/recall-tracking purposes — a dual-purpose documentation-integrity consideration relevant to HIM practice.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c04081ed-56a0-49dd-8966-2cdc77a9b13b', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 53, 'A patient''s chart contains two different admission dates/times in two different systems (registration system vs. clinical EHR timestamp) due to a system interface delay. For UHDDS/coding-abstraction accuracy, this should be:');
VALUES ('c04081ed-56a0-49dd-8966-2cdc77a9b13b', '802baec9-efaa-4d8f-9e2c-7aee36ba1de7', 'Admission date/time is itself a UHDDS data element; a known systems-interface discrepancy should be reconciled to the actual, clinically/legally accurate timestamp rather than left as an unresolved inconsistency simply because it originated from a technical artifact rather than a clinical documentation conflict.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('3e28ae55-3f2c-401b-b84e-e64bfc50b7e4', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 54, 'Which of the following best reflects the documentation standard for coding a ''debridement'' procedure''s depth (e.g., skin only vs. subcutaneous tissue vs. muscle vs. bone)?');
VALUES ('3e28ae55-3f2c-401b-b84e-e64bfc50b7e4', 'c860ee09-47ec-4137-872c-902f6b1b6a4f', 'Debridement coding (both ICD-10-PCS root operation/body part selection and CPT code selection in the outpatient/physician setting) is directly tied to documented depth — a well-known area of coding-documentation risk when operative notes describe technique but omit explicit depth.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('a68bd7a4-457f-4828-b60d-4fd4d29d03cd', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 55, 'A physician''s op note for a joint arthroplasty documents the procedure performed but never explicitly states whether it was a total or partial (unicompartmental) replacement — only implant supply documentation (from materials management) indicates a partial-knee implant system was used. The correct documentation-review approach is:');
VALUES ('a68bd7a4-457f-4828-b60d-4fd4d29d03cd', 'a7979cca-3978-4d04-92dc-db60df7ba9c2', 'While implant/supply data can be a useful cross-reference, it generally shouldn''t substitute for the performing physician''s own operative documentation establishing procedure extent — a genuine gap here (op note silent on extent) is appropriately closed via further note review or a compliant query, not by coding directly from a non-clinical supply log alone.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('c779640e-1ef2-401f-bb0b-159278d55d62', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 56, 'Which best describes the documentation-integrity concern with ''copy-paste'' carrying forward a prior day''s full physical exam findings into a new day''s progress note without updating for any interval change?');
VALUES ('c779640e-1ef2-401f-bb0b-159278d55d62', 'fb07bb2a-7e4f-46b9-8c5f-9472fef0937c', 'Copy-forward exam findings that don''t reflect true interval change risk the record misrepresenting a patient''s actual current status — both a patient-safety and a documentation-accuracy concern directly relevant to coding, which relies on the record accurately reflecting each specific encounter.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('28c06f8b-2c9d-48e7-88c5-cdaf52ac2d5f', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 57, 'A discharge summary states the patient''s ''discharge condition: stable'' while the last physician progress note (written the same day) describes ongoing significant symptoms and an unresolved acute issue. This apparent inconsistency should prompt:');
VALUES ('28c06f8b-2c9d-48e7-88c5-cdaf52ac2d5f', 'b49d673c-832b-46f2-8b8f-59776fa8d6bb', 'An apparent last-minute inconsistency should prompt review of the full surrounding documentation first (not just the two conflicting notes in isolation) — a compliant query can follow if genuine ambiguity remains, but the first step is always thorough record review, not an automatic assumption or automatic query.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('d160f7c5-0aba-487e-8857-bcfb5328ffc4', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 58, 'Which of the following best reflects the documentation standard needed to support coding a ''complication of anesthesia'' as a distinct reportable diagnosis?');
VALUES ('d160f7c5-0aba-487e-8857-bcfb5328ffc4', 'bc833b79-0650-4355-a0a8-698f28f052fd', 'Like other complication coding, an anesthesia-specific complication requires the provider to document the causal relationship — proximity in time to anesthesia administration alone is not sufficient documentation to support a complication code.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('8473115e-54ab-4fc4-a4be-dfb848cc1bfb', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 59, 'A patient''s problem list carries a diagnosis of ''chronic pain syndrome'' unchanged for several years across many encounters, while the current encounter''s actual documentation focuses entirely on an unrelated acute issue with no mention of pain management. Whether to report the chronic pain diagnosis for THIS encounter depends most on:');
VALUES ('8473115e-54ab-4fc4-a4be-dfb848cc1bfb', '0d7f7b57-7c91-4287-90e4-a1b5d7404d07', 'Problem lists are administrative conveniences, not automatic coding triggers — whether a listed chronic condition is reportable for a specific encounter still depends on whether it meets the relevant additional-diagnosis criteria for THAT encounter.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

VALUES ('01d95159-35f8-4145-ad1b-dcd48f3fe3e8', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 60, 'Which of the following best reflects the documentation expectation for coding ''blood transfusion reaction'' as a distinct diagnosis?');
VALUES ('01d95159-35f8-4145-ad1b-dcd48f3fe3e8', '8e916602-ac65-44ab-8c16-1e5fe0415faa', 'As with other complications, a transfusion reaction requires actual provider documentation of the clinical event — receiving a transfusion is not itself evidence that a reaction occurred, and monitoring documentation without a physician-documented reaction doesn''t establish the diagnosis on its own.', 'Domain 2 (Coding Documentation) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and standard health-record documentation-integrity guidance.');

-- ── Section: Domain 3 — Provider Queries (10 questions) — Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles). ──

VALUES ('62c14974-de7b-4259-a02e-7d7ccae558f6', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 61, 'A multiple-choice-format compliant query for clarifying the etiology of acute kidney injury should generally offer which type of answer set?');
VALUES ('62c14974-de7b-4259-a02e-7d7ccae558f6', '1744fc25-214b-41e9-a0a2-c71f5ef8fe08', 'A well-constructed multiple-choice query offers genuinely plausible clinical alternatives (not just the suspected answer) plus an ''other''/''undetermined'' option — this format keeps the query informative for the physician without steering them toward a predetermined conclusion.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('5320e6b9-2a8d-4da4-baef-076f658f5fe2', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 62, 'Per general query-compliance guidance, referencing clinical criteria (e.g., Sepsis-3 criteria, KDIGO AKI staging) within a query is:');
VALUES ('5320e6b9-2a8d-4da4-baef-076f658f5fe2', '85e65488-54f5-4c9d-914c-7f63b14403b9', 'Referencing recognized clinical criteria to explain the objective basis for a query is generally supportive of compliance (it grounds the query in real clinical evidence) — it becomes non-compliant only if used to lead the provider toward a specific predetermined answer rather than to present relevant clinical context.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('025108f0-85ca-4c88-8c3a-fb7a64717945', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 63, 'A provider responds to a compliant query by writing ''unable to determine'' for the underlying etiology of a lab abnormality. The appropriate coding action is:');
VALUES ('025108f0-85ca-4c88-8c3a-fb7a64717945', '12f772b0-cdcd-4dac-a449-11cb8cd706b5', 'A compliant ''clinically undetermined'' response is a legitimate clinical answer that must be respected — coding to the actual level of certainty the provider could establish, not overriding it with a guess or repeatedly pressuring for a more specific answer.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('fe2ce522-17ba-4000-ac26-8abfbea7aa5f', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 64, 'Which of the following most accurately reflects when a query should NOT be initiated?');
VALUES ('fe2ce522-17ba-4000-ac26-8abfbea7aa5f', '6980c6c4-580b-46e8-a533-eee146aed3dd', 'Querying when documentation is already clear and adequate — purely to seek a more favorable reimbursement outcome without genuine clinical ambiguity or missing specificity — is itself a compliance risk (''query fishing''), which is why real query-opportunity identification requires an actual documentation gap or ambiguity, not just a desire for a different code.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('48110d91-3c68-4385-bd72-b6c4f4885aa3', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 65, 'A CDI specialist drafts a query stating: ''The patient''s hemoglobin dropped from 13.5 to 8.2 g/dL postoperatively and 2 units of PRBCs were transfused. Please indicate the clinical significance of this finding: (a) acute blood loss anemia, (b) another specified condition, (c) not clinically significant.'' This query is best characterized as:');
VALUES ('48110d91-3c68-4385-bd72-b6c4f4885aa3', 'd07ba8dc-1b09-42f6-80fd-2007ef129330', 'Presenting the objective clinical indicators alongside multiple genuine options (including a ''not clinically significant'' option) is the hallmark of a well-constructed, non-leading multiple-choice query.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('6eb2eb70-d4c3-43b6-9317-c951be4f49f3', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 66, 'Which of the following is the most appropriate response to a physician who says, during an informal hallway conversation, ''just code it as sepsis, I don''t have time to document more''?');
VALUES ('6eb2eb70-d4c3-43b6-9317-c951be4f49f3', '925699ff-9f34-42e1-9e63-67f350ec0ddd', 'Coding must be based on documentation within the health record — an undocumented verbal instruction, however well-intentioned, doesn''t satisfy that requirement; the physician should be asked to document the clarification, even briefly, in the record itself.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('ee25110e-8a90-42c3-82c6-bc6aec993955', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 67, 'A query practice brief from AHIMA/ACDIS generally recommends that queries avoid which of the following formatting practices?');
VALUES ('ee25110e-8a90-42c3-82c6-bc6aec993955', '11347af2-bc56-4eab-a2c0-36d52f31ab21', 'Query best practice guidance consistently flags embedding the desired answer within the question itself as the core marker of a leading, non-compliant query — the opposite of presenting indicators/options and letting the provider reach an independent conclusion.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('c1c675ae-e32a-4696-8008-6eba75bbd98d', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 68, 'A coder identifies that a chart has clinical indicators strongly suggestive of malnutrition (low albumin, significant unintentional weight loss, physical wasting documented by a dietitian) but no physician diagnosis of malnutrition anywhere in the record. The appropriate next step is most likely:');
VALUES ('c1c675ae-e32a-4696-8008-6eba75bbd98d', 'a0e62b2a-fb88-49cf-ae2c-71e8c0f8a220', 'This is a textbook example of a genuine query opportunity: real clinical indicators exist, but only a provider can establish the diagnosis itself — the coder''s job is to recognize the gap and construct a compliant query, not to code from ancillary documentation alone or ignore the signal.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('9df83ec6-84e9-41c5-b441-9360cc56f4a3', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 69, 'Which best reflects how a coder should handle a situation where a query has been sent but the provider has not responded by the time of claim submission deadlines?');
VALUES ('9df83ec6-84e9-41c5-b441-9360cc56f4a3', '84216762-8498-4d6a-9afb-e4759e87282a', 'Organizations generally have query-escalation/timeliness policies for exactly this scenario — the coder shouldn''t invent an assumed answer, but also shouldn''t necessarily hold every claim indefinitely; following the organization''s defined escalation process is the compliant path.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

VALUES ('0d69dcba-f2b5-49cf-8017-2ac58665bec5', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 70, 'Which best describes the purpose of tracking query response rates and query-to-diagnosis-change outcomes as part of a compliance/CDI program''s ongoing monitoring?');
VALUES ('0d69dcba-f2b5-49cf-8017-2ac58665bec5', '9b583179-1ff6-47ad-af50-8c6ef5a0ac67', 'Query outcome/response-rate tracking is a recognized program-monitoring tool — unusually skewed patterns (e.g., near-100% agreement on severity-increasing queries) can itself be a compliance red flag worth reviewing for query-construction quality, not just a productivity metric.', 'Domain 3 (Provider Queries) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline and AHIMA/ACDIS query-compliance guidance (non-leading query principles).');

-- ── Section: Domain 4 — Regulatory Compliance (20 questions) — Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts. ──

VALUES ('bdfdf08c-14f5-4c56-9bc5-544733be2f80', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 71, 'A Recovery Audit Contractor (RAC) audit is best described as:');
VALUES ('bdfdf08c-14f5-4c56-9bc5-544733be2f80', '9b2340b6-335c-4707-b4f4-17a03a7de5ec', 'RACs are CMS-contracted auditors reviewing claims for improper payments, including coding accuracy issues — understanding RAC audit exposure is a standard regulatory-compliance knowledge area for credentialed coders.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('59337d79-28f2-4749-a3dc-c7d13507c5cb', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 72, 'PSI-90 (the CMS Patient Safety and Adverse Events Composite) aggregates multiple individual Patient Safety Indicators into a single composite measure used for:');
VALUES ('59337d79-28f2-4749-a3dc-c7d13507c5cb', 'e908ad74-b712-4b79-bc40-bfbaab16a7a9', 'PSI-90 rolls multiple PSIs into a composite hospital-level quality/safety measure tied to CMS quality reporting and value-based payment programs — accurate underlying coding (including POA) is what makes this composite measure meaningful.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('249bf297-3535-4218-a782-6108e057857d', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 73, 'Which best describes the relationship between accurate POA indicator assignment and a hospital''s PSI/HAC-related quality metrics and payment adjustments?');
VALUES ('249bf297-3535-4218-a782-6108e057857d', '59794259-41c8-4c2b-a319-068ad712f652', 'POA assignment is the direct data element PSI/HAC logic depends on to distinguish preexisting conditions from hospital-acquired ones — this is precisely why POA accuracy carries real regulatory/financial and quality-reporting weight, not just an administrative data point.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('26310805-83cc-4725-8605-7275e7bb7509', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 74, 'An OIG (Office of Inspector General) Work Plan item flags increased scrutiny of a specific DRG or coding pattern nationally. From a facility compliance standpoint, an appropriate response includes:');
VALUES ('26310805-83cc-4725-8605-7275e7bb7509', '7c5d24d2-8fc1-44d6-96fb-2a64b799da28', 'OIG Work Plan publications, while not binding rules themselves, are a recognized signal of upcoming federal audit focus areas — proactive internal review of flagged patterns is a standard, recommended compliance response.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('90e07fb3-37de-43e7-b8d9-7688bc850bbc', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 75, 'Which of the following best reflects a compliant approach to coding for a payer requiring prior authorization for a given procedure, where the authorization was never actually obtained?');
VALUES ('90e07fb3-37de-43e7-b8d9-7688bc850bbc', 'e9ccbdf1-71c1-4cf6-b57b-7d062f47ac45', 'Prior authorization status is a payer/administrative billing matter — it should never drive a coder to select a diagnosis/procedure code inconsistent with actual clinical documentation just to avoid an authorization-related denial; that would itself be a compliance violation (inaccurate coding to manipulate claim outcome).', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('91c8a4e6-c0e6-482a-906c-04eec14b5520', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 76, 'A Comprehensive Error Rate Testing (CERT) program review is best described as:');
VALUES ('91c8a4e6-c0e6-482a-906c-04eec14b5520', '8100ad7e-314b-4d7b-bf67-0cd11742cb2f', 'CERT is CMS''s ongoing improper-payment-rate measurement program — understanding its existence and role (alongside RAC, MAC, and OIG activities) rounds out a CCS candidate''s regulatory-compliance-program knowledge.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('c7a06b1c-e659-4062-a07e-baebb36367c1', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 77, 'A Medicare Administrative Contractor (MAC) is responsible for which of the following, among other functions relevant to coding/billing compliance?');
VALUES ('c7a06b1c-e659-4062-a07e-baebb36367c1', '85bd7aa5-0d9a-4d6a-9a3e-e9d3709a8f69', 'MACs process Medicare claims for their jurisdiction and issue LCDs — payer-specific (though regionally-scoped, CMS-derived) medical necessity policies that interact directly with the diagnosis-to-procedure medical necessity edits discussed elsewhere in Domain 4.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('631af4db-bae4-447d-b98c-be456b7f63ef', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 78, 'A National Coverage Determination (NCD) differs from a Local Coverage Determination (LCD) primarily in that:');
VALUES ('631af4db-bae4-447d-b98c-be456b7f63ef', '1d7880ac-8931-4822-ad42-7ba9f61829c4', 'NCDs are national CMS coverage policies; LCDs are MAC-specific regional coverage policies — both can drive medical necessity edits, but at different scopes, and an NCD generally takes precedence over a conflicting LCD.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('e0eeaef8-962f-4a13-b78a-e33a81b5c2b1', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 79, 'Which best describes a compliant approach when a coder discovers, during routine work (not a formal audit), that a specific diagnosis code has been incorrectly and systematically applied to a whole category of claims for several months due to an encoder configuration error?');
VALUES ('e0eeaef8-962f-4a13-b78a-e33a81b5c2b1', '75eccb1b-5d54-42c3-a41b-e8ac7cc8e277', 'A systemic error discovered through any means (not just formal audit) should be escalated through compliance channels — proactive identification and correction (including potential self-disclosure for significant historical overpayments) is the compliant response, not silence or informal quiet fixes.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('c13d93da-d52f-45af-9403-2eb89f3dc8be', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 80, 'The 60-day overpayment rule under the Affordable Care Act, relevant to coding/billing compliance, generally requires:');
VALUES ('c13d93da-d52f-45af-9403-2eb89f3dc8be', 'c1e4db5f-4824-490f-9542-39d402695948', 'The 60-day rule creates an affirmative obligation to report/return identified overpayments within a defined window — failing to do so once an overpayment is identified (including one traced to a systemic coding error) creates its own compliance exposure.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('202f579f-1050-4674-9689-f243b31d548d', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 81, 'Which best describes ''unbundling'' as a compliance risk in coding?');
VALUES ('202f579f-1050-4674-9689-f243b31d548d', 'd1172d9b-a3de-4390-a73b-283a6a6c757b', 'Unbundling — separately billing components of a procedure that should be captured by one comprehensive code — is a classic improper-payment pattern targeted by NCCI PTP edits and a recurring OIG/RAC audit focus area.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('e5a5f79c-fb2a-4022-bc1c-ff78c6c9e81d', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 82, 'Which best describes a compliant coder''s obligation when internal productivity pressure (e.g., a high daily quota) makes it tempting to skip verifying ambiguous documentation and simply assign a ''reasonable guess'' code?');
VALUES ('e5a5f79c-fb2a-4022-bc1c-ff78c6c9e81d', '272dcb64-0d3f-474a-bdd0-32c0da8bf68c', 'AHIMA''s ethical standards and general compliance principles hold that productivity pressure does not excuse assigning unsupported/guessed codes — the professional obligation to code accurately based on documentation doesn''t yield to internal metrics, and concerns about unrealistic expectations should be raised, not resolved by cutting corners.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('f5b833fa-f26c-4552-8c8c-66ce57458bd5', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 83, 'A hospital''s compliance officer conducts a root-cause analysis after a coding-related billing error is discovered. Which best reflects an appropriate scope for that analysis?');
VALUES ('f5b833fa-f26c-4552-8c8c-66ce57458bd5', 'e79987e5-e037-4b5b-a4bb-958bf4816f2f', 'Effective root-cause analysis looks beyond the individual action to systemic contributing factors — this is standard compliance-program practice and distinguishes a genuine corrective-action process from simple individual blame.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('744263ab-7d7b-4ebb-b6b7-3a6524328bdf', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 84, 'Which of the following best reflects the relationship between the HIPAA Security Rule''s risk analysis requirement and coding department operations?');
VALUES ('744263ab-7d7b-4ebb-b6b7-3a6524328bdf', 'edde07d9-d1fa-47b5-bbc3-291a37efae7b', 'The Security Rule''s risk analysis requirement is ongoing and covers all systems/workflows touching ePHI — coding/HIM systems and any remote-access arrangements for coding staff are squarely within that scope, not a carve-out.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('21af6c6c-edfb-4bda-8473-eb8a2999c6e3', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 85, 'Which of the following best describes appropriate remote/telework access controls for coding staff who access ePHI from outside the facility, from a compliance standpoint?');
VALUES ('21af6c6c-edfb-4bda-8473-eb8a2999c6e3', '9dcf8e8e-753b-43d7-b1b8-904d45bcb83b', 'Remote/telework ePHI access introduces distinct risks the Security Rule''s risk-based framework requires addressing with appropriate technical/administrative safeguards — not treating remote access as equivalent-risk to on-site access with no additional controls.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('5a6be920-9ef9-4d02-b174-9b5132093ae3', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 86, 'A coding compliance policy requiring periodic re-certification/competency validation for credentialed coders (beyond the initial CCS credential) primarily serves to:');
VALUES ('5a6be920-9ef9-4d02-b174-9b5132093ae3', 'c6fdda7b-c283-45f8-a343-e9bc727a11f3', 'Because code sets, guidelines, and payer/regulatory requirements evolve continuously (annual ICD-10-CM/PCS updates, guideline changes, new NCCI edits), ongoing competency validation — not just an initial credential — is a recognized best practice supporting sustained coding accuracy and compliance.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('35691a69-f607-467c-b699-ec0668f13fa1', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 87, 'Which best describes the compliance significance of a hospital consistently having a much higher CC/MCC capture rate than similar peer facilities, with no clear clinical explanation for the difference?');
VALUES ('35691a69-f607-467c-b699-ec0668f13fa1', '94fff15e-3f4e-4d94-9951-13d4724e5192', 'An outlier data pattern like this warrants proactive internal verification — neither assuming the worst (fraud) nor assuming the best (pure skill) without actually checking that the underlying documentation and coding practices support the observed rate.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('f1b41028-7dd6-4375-a4dd-25e8a99dcba7', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 88, 'Which of the following best reflects the compliance principle behind ''coding to the highest level of specificity supported by documentation,'' as distinguished from ''coding to the highest-reimbursing code available''?');
VALUES ('f1b41028-7dd6-4375-a4dd-25e8a99dcba7', '7e853802-8893-41f4-8708-2396f614f03d', 'The core compliance distinction: specificity should follow the documentation, not the other way around — selecting a higher-reimbursing option specifically because of its reimbursement impact, when documentation doesn''t clearly support it over another equally plausible code, is the compliance risk this principle is meant to prevent.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('3b7132b7-d546-4062-a5cb-ab5ba1a1244a', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 89, 'A facility''s compliance training program for coders should, at minimum, address which of the following areas together?');
VALUES ('3b7132b7-d546-4062-a5cb-ab5ba1a1244a', '106e5af9-610c-4a4f-b774-202fe7774475', 'A well-rounded compliance training program spans code-set currency, ethical standards, fraud/abuse law awareness, privacy/security obligations, and internal policy — not a single narrow topic, and not a one-time-only new-hire event.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

VALUES ('21064cb9-66dc-456a-8a95-a7736081718e', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 90, 'Which best reflects the compliance rationale for maintaining a clear separation of duties between coding staff and staff who determine final claim reimbursement amounts/negotiate payer contracts?');
VALUES ('21064cb9-66dc-456a-8a95-a7736081718e', 'ce85d99b-99b5-48c0-94b3-b09d5dfa56cc', 'Separation-of-duties principles applied to coding vs. reimbursement-negotiation functions help protect coding-accuracy integrity from potential (even unintentional) reimbursement-driven pressure — a recognized internal-control concept relevant to healthcare compliance programs generally.', 'Domain 4 (Regulatory Compliance) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline, HIPAA Privacy/Security Rule basics, and CMS PSI/HAC/RAC program concepts.');

-- ── Section: Domain 5 — Information Technologies (10 questions) — Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA's CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH. ──

VALUES ('ef3d2827-ab1c-43c4-ba71-59f7079e9962', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 91, 'A Health Information Exchange (HIE) primarily enables:');
VALUES ('ef3d2827-ab1c-43c4-ba71-59f7079e9962', '3b04747e-95f9-43f1-95aa-3bbc15cf1947', 'HIEs are built specifically to enable secure health information sharing ACROSS organizations (often unaffiliated ones) — a practical expression of the broader EHR interoperability concept, distinct from a single organization''s internal EMR.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('40ac3387-e23d-49c5-8cc3-737a66a97053', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 92, 'Which best describes a coder/HIM professional''s role regarding data integrity within EHR systems, from an information-technology standpoint?');
VALUES ('40ac3387-e23d-49c5-8cc3-737a66a97053', 'd5dc9d12-4bda-40cb-92ac-60e6411b4f19', 'EHR-specific documentation risks (copy-forward errors propagating outdated/inaccurate information, cloned/templated notes overstating exam findings) are a recognized HIM/coding-adjacent data-integrity concern, not purely an IT infrastructure issue.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('97668507-59c8-4cd8-8fca-5604e7ca7be0', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 93, 'Which of the following best describes the purpose of a Master Patient Index (MPI) within a health information system?');
VALUES ('97668507-59c8-4cd8-8fca-5604e7ca7be0', 'cc6d6b6e-5725-49f2-b40e-d6122463c71a', 'An MPI''s core function is accurate patient identification/matching across encounters and systems — poor MPI matching (duplicate records or incorrectly merged records) is a known real-world HIM data-integrity risk with downstream coding/continuity-of-care consequences.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('e08969cc-9f86-446e-94eb-8ffc97d7bd7a', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 94, 'Natural Language Processing (NLP), as used in CAC systems, functions by:');
VALUES ('e08969cc-9f86-446e-94eb-8ffc97d7bd7a', '8f620d75-0f81-4ed4-9ee2-fd1d4c664e1d', 'NLP is the underlying technology that lets CAC systems parse unstructured free-text documentation and surface clinically relevant concepts as candidate codes — still requiring coder review/validation, as covered in Domain 5''s CAC-related content.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('41584bb6-b50f-41dd-aed0-c71d53143d02', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 95, 'A facility considering adoption of a new encoder/CAC platform should evaluate interoperability with which of the following, among other systems, to support an effective coding workflow?');
VALUES ('41584bb6-b50f-41dd-aed0-c71d53143d02', '2baf6d4d-f58e-433b-95d9-5301f66dd7a9', 'A coding-support platform''s practical value depends heavily on how well it integrates with the clinical documentation source (EHR), the abstracting workflow, and downstream billing systems — poor interoperability here is a real operational and data-integrity risk, not just a convenience issue.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('878abfc0-8e84-49eb-965a-9d1507e768ac', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 96, 'Which of the following best describes ''audit trail'' functionality within an EHR system, as relevant to HIM/coding compliance?');
VALUES ('878abfc0-8e84-49eb-965a-9d1507e768ac', 'cdbd376b-f347-404b-a297-0a9af58ada83', 'Audit trails serve both security (detecting unauthorized/inappropriate access) and documentation-integrity purposes (establishing when entries and amendments were actually made) — both directly relevant to HIM and coding compliance oversight.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('093042ee-1b7f-4dec-856d-1832442518a2', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 97, 'Which best describes the concept of ''data granularity'' as it relates to structured EHR data feeding into CAC or analytics systems?');
VALUES ('093042ee-1b7f-4dec-856d-1832442518a2', '1ef0cb52-a650-4c2d-bb4d-ffe337409b2d', 'Data granularity — how specific/discrete captured data elements are — directly affects downstream usability for CAC suggestion accuracy, quality measure calculation, and analytics; coarser, free-text-only data is harder for both CAC/NLP and structured reporting to use reliably.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('af8274a3-29b8-477f-a671-81ac9fc47c39', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 98, 'A facility migrating from one EHR/HIM platform to another faces a data-mapping challenge where the old system''s diagnosis code field allowed free text while the new system requires structured, coded entries. Which best describes the primary HIM/coding-relevant risk here?');
VALUES ('af8274a3-29b8-477f-a671-81ac9fc47c39', '649e7810-df4a-4f8a-80c3-68b730b81b6c', 'Legacy free-text-to-structured-code migrations are a real, well-known data-integrity risk requiring careful validation — exactly the kind of legacy-system interoperability challenge HIM professionals should understand and be involved in reviewing, not a pure IT-only concern.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('ca379f6a-d96b-4a01-aa9c-aa921753d1b1', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 99, 'Which of the following best describes a ''clinical terminology'' standard like SNOMED CT, as distinguished from a classification system like ICD-10-CM?');
VALUES ('ca379f6a-d96b-4a01-aa9c-aa921753d1b1', 'dd117215-5a09-4d6d-b5d5-382693e8d691', 'SNOMED CT and ICD-10-CM serve related but distinct purposes — reference terminology for granular clinical representation vs. classification for statistical/administrative grouping — and understanding this distinction (including cross-mapping efforts between them) is a relevant HIM information-technology concept.', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');

VALUES ('ffca57b0-08c3-4bc3-86c0-320a492c5557', '21f20bd3-a154-4a3d-a4ce-f2fbba93b3f7', 100, 'Which best reflects a sound HIM governance practice regarding a facility''s encoder/CAC/grouper software updates when annual ICD-10-CM/PCS code set updates are released?');
VALUES ('ffca57b0-08c3-4bc3-86c0-320a492c5557', '82a14718-f9d6-4bb9-910d-7a90c3402880', 'Coordinated, validated software updates timed to annual code-set changes are a core HIM/IT governance responsibility — assuming automatic vendor accuracy without facility-side validation is a real operational risk (e.g., a code newly invalid or newly required not being correctly reflected in edit logic).', 'Domain 5 (Information Technologies) — authored from AHIMA''s CCS Exam Content Outline covering EHR interoperability, encoder/grouper software, CAC, and HITECH.');
